1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:01:01,227
There are places in America where the old works are still visible. A mine entrance cut into a mountain. A power plant standing silent beside a river. A factory gate through which generations once walked to work. The building remains. The people remain. But the livelihood around which an entire community was built has been deliberately removed. A job is not merely a stream of income. It can be the cornerstone beneath a life. A worker builds knowledge around it. A family builds its home around it. Local businesses build their customers around it. A community builds its schools, churches, friendships, and expectations around it. When that cornerstone is removed, the damage is not measured by one missing paycheck. Government often changes policy, and economic conditions change with it. The Cornerstone does not promise a public remedy every time technology advances, consumers choose something new, or an industry loses a fair competition. The policy has a narrower trigger.

2
00:01:01,227 --> 00:01:51,427
The Cornerstone applies when government expressly makes the destruction or material contraction of a lawful industry an objective—or an intended means—of public policy; when government acts to accomplish that objective; and when those actions directly destroy the livelihoods of identifiable people. Coal miners provide a clear example. An administration may believe that replacing coal serves an environmental or economic purpose. It may possess lawful authority to pursue that policy. But if it intentionally chooses the destruction of coal employment as the means of achieving its goal, it cannot designate particular workers to bear the cost and then dismiss them as collateral damage. Government may change policy. It may not deliberately sacrifice citizens and walk away. The Cornerstone begins with certification.

3
00:01:51,427 --> 00:02:33,297
Under fixed rules established by Congress, the appropriate public institutions will examine the government’s action, the evidence of intent, the affected industry, the worksites and occupations covered, the period during which the policy caused the losses, and the people directly displaced. The policy is not limited to a particular department. The institutional assignments will follow the expertise and lawful authority required to perform each function. What matters is that the standards remain fixed, the evidence public, the process reviewable, and political officials unable to decide whose injury deserves recognition. Eligibility will remain direct and individual.

4
00:02:33,297 --> 00:03:31,483
An employee, independent worker, or owner-operator whose principal livelihood depended upon the targeted work can qualify when the certified policy directly caused its loss. A supplier or contractor can qualify when the targeted industry supplied the principal share of that person’s work and the causal connection is proven. General regional decline will not be enough. A distant loss of customers will not be enough. The Cornerstone repairs identifiable livelihoods deliberately destroyed by government. It does not become a general economic-development program with limitless boundaries. A worker will not lose eligibility merely because he later found another job, moved away, received unemployment benefits, or rebuilt his life through exceptional effort. Resilience does not excuse the original wrong. Once the affected workers have been certified, they will form voluntary Cornerstone Associations. The government will not choose a replacement business for them.

5
00:03:31,483 --> 00:04:27,833
The workers will decide. Every founding member will receive one vote. Every founding member will receive an equal ownership interest in the association’s common economic rights. Every member will have access to the books, the compact, investor agreements, and independent audits. The association will exist because thousands of separate workers cannot sensibly divide one regional commercial right into thousands of isolated pieces. It will give them the scale to build something powerful without taking decision-making power away from any of them. The members will examine the opportunities available. They will debate them. They will vote. They will decide what they want to build together. No worker will be forced to retrain, relocate, accept a job in the new enterprise, or assume personal debt. A member who has already found satisfying work somewhere else may keep that job and retain an ownership voice in the association.

6
00:04:27,833 --> 00:05:22,274
Members who want direct participation will receive first opportunity for funded training, qualified employment, or a member franchise created by the new enterprise. The economic opportunity will come from states that choose to participate. State governments prohibit many industries or trades within their borders. When a state prepares to open one of those industries for the first time, it may offer a Cornerstone Association a limited regional monopoly: an exclusive right, for a fixed period and within a specified district, to enter that newly lawful market. The offer must pass the same essential test that governs The Garden: justice for one person cannot be built by committing a new injustice against another. If a worker, business, license holder, contractor, customer, applicant, or lawful competitor already occupies or competes for that economic position, the offer cannot be used.

7
00:05:22,274 --> 00:06:18,770
The state will restore a liberty previously withheld from everyone in that district. It will not confiscate a market somebody else built. Participating states will publish the opportunities they are prepared to offer. Cornerstone Associations will submit sealed, ranked choices showing which opportunities they are willing to pursue. If two associations choose different opportunities, each proceeds toward its preferred negotiation. If two associations seek the same opportunity and cannot share it or form a consortium, a fixed public rule will resolve the conflict. Priority will go to the oldest certified injury that remains unrepaired. If the claims are equally old, a public random drawing will break the tie. No official will rank which workers are more sympathetic. No party will purchase priority. No administration will reward allies or punish critics. The rule will decide, and the public will see how it decided.

8
00:06:18,770 --> 00:07:12,671
Once an association is matched with an opportunity, the workers and the participating state will negotiate the compact. The compact will define the industry, district, duration, operating conditions, public protections, and the exact commercial rights the association receives. The workers will retain voting control. Private investors may provide capital through loans, nonvoting equity, or revenue interests. They may earn a fair return for taking risk. But they will not buy the compact, seize the association’s votes, acquire a hidden veto, or transfer the public right to someone else. Government will receive no board seat, golden share, or management authority. It will supervise through transparent law—not through quiet control of the company. Independent state regulators will enforce safety, labor, environmental, consumer, and anti-corruption rules just as they do elsewhere.

9
00:07:12,671 --> 00:07:57,158
A revolving Cornerstone Founding Fund will finance the work required before ordinary investors can responsibly enter: certification, worker enrollment, association formation, independent feasibility studies, legal and accounting preparation, licensing, and member training. No individual worker will owe that money personally. Once the enterprise begins earning revenue, the association will repay the Founding Fund under a fixed formula established when the compact is signed. When the public advance and fixed return have been repaid, the obligation will expire permanently. The recovered capital will then repair the next certified injury. And government will not satisfy its debt with a hollow piece of paper.

10
00:07:57,158 --> 00:08:57,286
Before the remedy is considered delivered, the necessary authorizations must be legally effective. The compact must be enforceable. The enterprise must pass an independent feasibility review. Members must have access to funded training. And the association must possess a lawful path to private capital. After that, commercial risk belongs to the owners. The Cornerstone will not guarantee profit. It will not guarantee that every business decision succeeds. It will not reopen every closed mine or reverse every underlying policy. It will provide something both more limited and more meaningful: a real field in which effort can matter again. Some will object that this policy stands in the way of progress. It does not. Government will remain free to pursue new energy, new technology, environmental protection, or any other lawful public objective. But progress purchased by intentionally destroying particular citizens’ livelihoods creates a debt to those citizens.

11
00:08:57,286 --> 00:09:00,286
Paying that debt does not stop progress. It makes progress just. The old works may remain closed. Their towers, gates, and machinery may remain visible on the horizon as evidence of what that community once built. But the workers will not remain abandoned beside them. They will possess a new enterprise that they chose. They will own it together. They will govern it together. They will decide what to build upon the opportunity government returned. Justice does not require us to estimate the dollar value of a person’s lost identity, community, and future and then mail a check. It requires us to replace the foundation that government deliberately removed. When government takes the cornerstone from beneath a citizen’s life, it owes that citizen a new foundation. The Cornerstone will deliver it. Then the workers will decide what rises upon it.
